    "lawCode": "Amd §3201, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A10038\n\nTITLE  OF  BILL:    An  act  to  amend the insurance law, in relation to\napproval of certain insurance and annuity policy forms\n\nPURPOSE: This bill would amend the insurance law to  permit  the  Super-\nintendent  of  Insurance  to  waive the re-approval of an amended policy\nform when the amendment is due to a revision to a separate  account,  so\nlong as the revision has already been approved by the Department as part\nof  the  separate  account plan of operation. Under those circumstances,\nsuch amended policy form would need to  be  filed  on  an  informational\nbasis  with  the  New York State Insurance Department, within sixty days\nafter approval of the amended  separate  account  plan.  This  amendment\nwould  also  give  the  Superintendent  the  discretion  to  waive other\napprovals of amended policy forms.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  This bill establishes a  new  paragraph  (7)  to\n53201(b)  of the insurance law to authorize the Superintendent of Insur-\nance to waive the approval of an amended policy form,  so  long  as  the\namendment  had  already  been re-approved by the Insurance Department as\npart of a separate account plan of operation and so long as the  amended\nform  is  filed with the Insurance Department on an informational basis,\nwithin sixty days after the revised separate account plan  is  approved.\nCurrent  law  is being interpreted so as not to allow the Department any\ndiscretion in waiving the re-approval of these types of  amended  policy\nforms.   This  amendment  would  further  give  the  Superintendent  the\ndiscretion to waive the approval of any type of amendment  to  a  policy\nform.  Current  law  would  not  seem  to  allow the Superintendent this\ndiscretion in this regard.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: Interpretation of the current law has led to the  conclu-\nsion by some that the Insurance Department has little discretion when it\ncomes  to  determining  whether  or not an amendment to a previously-ap-\nproved policy form rises to the level  of  needing  re-approval  by  the\nInsurance  Department.  This has become an impediment when insurers need\nto make simple revisions to policies or contracts, particularly variable\nlife and annuity contracts that contain  a  separate  account.  Separate\naccounts  are mutual fund-type options that are offered as an investment\noption under certain variable insurance  products.  These  accounts  are\nadministered  by  another  company (such as Fidelity or Magellan), which\nwill routinely make fund revisions (such as,  a  simple  renaming  of  a\nfund).  Revisions  to  a  separate  account  under  a variable insurance\ncontract are already required to be reviewed and reapproved pursuant  to\nthe  separate account plan of operation required pursuant to 54240(e) of\nthe Insurance Law. Currently, the  Insurance  Department  also  requires\nthat  the amended policy form itself must also be reviewed and approved,\nin addition to the approval of the  amended  separate  account  plan  of\noperation. This dual-track, re-approval process unnecessarily slows down\nthe  ability  of  insurers to turn around amended forms to the insurance\nmarketplace. This revision to the law would allow the Superintendent the\ndiscretion to waive approvals  of  amended  policy  forms,  specifically\nunder the circumstances described above, but also more generally, if the\n\nDepartment  determines  any amendment is inconsequential enough so as to\nnot rise to the level of-needing to be re-approved.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: None.\n\nFISCAL  IMPLICATIONS:  No  known  costs to the State are associated with\nthis bill.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect sixty  days  after  it  shall\nhave become a law.\n\n",
